Cassandra (NoSQL - Wide-Column Store)
Think of Cassandra like a big notebook, where each page (row) can have different notes (columns).

It splits and spreads data across many computers so no single computer holds everything.

Instead of traditional indexes, it uses a list of sorted notes (LSM Trees + SSTables) to find things faster.

Imagine a Grocery List -

1️⃣ Writing Quickly (LSM Tree - Log-Structured Merge Tree)
You are making a grocery list but are in a hurry.

Instead of organizing it neatly, you just write down items randomly on a sticky note.

You keep doing this every time you remember something (keep adding new data).

2️⃣ Organizing Later (SSTables - Sorted Storage Tables)
At the end of the day, you take all your sticky notes and copy the items neatly onto a new, organized list (sorted alphabetically).

Once written, you never change this list—you just add new ones later when needed.

3️⃣ Searching Fast (Indexing & Lookup)
When you need "Milk," instead of checking every sticky note, you go to your latest sorted list and find it quickly.

Since it's already organized, you don’t waste time searching randomly.

How is this Different from Traditional Storage?
Cassandra (LSM + SSTables) → Writes first, organizes later → Fast writing ✅

Traditional Indexes (Like MySQL’s B+ Tree) → Organizes immediately while writing → Slower writing ❌

This is why Cassandra is super fast at writing data compared to databases that organize everything immediately.

